   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 451 of the laws of 2012, amending the labor law\nrelating to permitted deductions from wages, in relation to extending the\neffectiveness of such provisions",
    "basePrintNo": "S8834",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8834-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3, Chap 451 of 2012",
    "lawSection": "Labor",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8834\n\nSPONSOR: YOUNG\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend chapter 451 of the laws of 2012,\namending the labor law relating to permitted deductions from wages, in\nrelation to extending the effectiveness of such provisions\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend the existing provisions in Labor\nLaw § 193 for two years. Under current law, these provisions will expire\non November 6, 2018 and the law will revert back to a prior version of\nLabor Law § 193.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill would extend the expiration date of Labor Law §\n193 to November 6, 2020.\n\nSection two of the bill would make it effective immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nLabor Law § 193 sets forth the categories of permissible wage deductions\nthat may be taken by employers with the consent of employees. This\nsection also provides for use of wage deductions to recapture overpay-\nments of wages due to clerical or mathematical error and for repayment\nof advances on wages paid to employees.\n\nBefore the current version of Labor Law § 193 was enacted, allowable\nwage deductions were limited to those deductions otherwise permitted by\nlaw (e.g., tax withholdings) or to specific categories of commonly\nrecognized deductions such as retirement savings, healthcare insurance,\nand other traditional benefits that did not include deductions for goods\nand services.\n\nBoth employers and employees have expressed support for the expanded\nlist of permissible deductions as it exists today, which includes\ndeductions for service providers like health clubs, day care centers,\nand parking vendors. Extending these changes to the labor law for an\nadditional two years to permit employees to consent to these additional\ntypes of deductions ensures that the benefits associated with such\nvoluntary deductions may continue.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nLabor Law § 193 was first enacted in its current form in 2012 (L.2012,\nc.451; Governor's Program Bill  49) and extended once in 2015 (L.2015,\nc.386).\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n",
